---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
---

<GuideLayout slug="viewmaxxing-on-pc">
  <div class="gx-guide">
    <div class="gx-hero">
      <p class="gx-hero__kicker">No box? No problem.</p>
      <p class="gx-hero__line">
        Try the new <strong>PC web beta</strong> in your browser, or run the Android TV app in a free
        emulator when you want the same remote-first box experience on Windows.
      </p>
      <div class="gx-glance">
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">⏱</span> ~10 min</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">💵</span> $0</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🎮</span> Easy</span>
        <span class="gx-chip"><span class="gx-chip__i">🖥</span> Windows PC</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-intro">
      Viewmaxxing is built for <strong>VSeeBox V3, SuperBox V3, and other compatible Android TV boxes</strong>.
      It folds <strong>YouTube, Twitch, movies, TV and anime</strong> into one remote-first app. The new PC
      web version is in beta at <a href="https://view.maxxtopia.com" target="_blank" style="color: #10b981; font-weight: bold; text-decoration: underline;">view.maxxtopia.com</a>
      and needs no install; the APK route below is for running the box build on Windows.
    </p>

    <h2>⚡ Why it just works</h2>
    <ul class="gx-checks">
      <li><strong>It's a standard APK.</strong> The current build supports Android 8+; use an emulator image at Android 8 or newer.</li>
      <li><strong>It is D-pad-first.</strong> Arrow keys, a controller, or the mouse can drive the same focus-based layout that a TV remote uses.</li>
      <li><strong>The web beta is the quick route.</strong> Open it in a browser with no emulator; use the APK route when you specifically want the Android TV build.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>🛠 Run the box app on Windows — 4 steps</h2>
    <div class="gx-steps">
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">1</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Install an emulator</h3>
          <p>Grab <strong>BlueStacks 5</strong> from its official site (easiest). On a low-spec PC,
            <strong>LDPlayer 9</strong> is lighter. No Google login needed.</p>
          <p class="gx-note">👉 If setup offers <strong>virtualization (VT-x / AMD-V)</strong>, turn it on. It's the #1 thing that makes it smooth vs. sluggish.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">2</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Grab the APK</h3>
          <p>Join the Maxxtopia Discord → open <span class="gx-tag">#free-stuff</span> → download the latest
            <code>.apk</code> to your Desktop. This is the Android TV build for the box route.</p>
          <p class="gx-note">🔗 <strong>discord.gg/S78eecbWdx</strong> — the app's updater can handle later releases, so you only sideload the APK once.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">3</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Drag it in</h3>
          <p>With the emulator open, <strong>drag the <code>.apk</code> onto the window</strong> and drop.
            The icon appears in a few seconds.</p>
          <p class="gx-note">🧩 Drag not working? Use the <strong>Install APK</strong> button in the side toolbar (BlueStacks: under "System apps").</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-step">
        <div class="gx-step__num">4</div>
        <div class="gx-step__body">
          <h3>Open it &amp; play</h3>
          <p>Launch viewmaxxing. Home paints with the tab bar up top. Go <strong>full screen</strong>
            (BlueStacks: <span class="gx-key">F11</span>) for the couch feel — it's landscape already.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>🎮 The controls</h2>
    <p>Remote-first, which on PC is a <em>good</em> thing — the defaults already work, no custom setup:</p>
    <div class="gx-controls">
      <div class="gx-ctrl">
        <div class="gx-ctrl__keys">
          <span class="gx-key">↑</span><span class="gx-key">↓</span><span class="gx-key">←</span><span class="gx-key">→</span>
        </div>
        <span class="gx-ctrl__label">Move around</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-ctrl">
        <div class="gx-ctrl__keys"><span class="gx-key gx-key--wide">Enter</span></div>
        <span class="gx-ctrl__label">Select / play</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-ctrl">
        <div class="gx-ctrl__keys"><span class="gx-key gx-key--wide">Esc</span></div>
        <span class="gx-ctrl__label">Go back</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-ctrl">
        <div class="gx-ctrl__keys"><span class="gx-key">🖱</span></div>
        <span class="gx-ctrl__label">Click anything</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gx-callout gx-callout--cyan">
      <p class="gx-callout__tag">⌨️ No arrow keys? (60% boards, laptops)</p>
      <p>Totally fine — arrows are just the convenient default. Any one of these drives the whole app:</p>
      <ul class="gx-mini">
        <li><strong>Mouse only</strong> — everything's clickable. Zero keys. Simplest answer.</li>
        <li><strong>Remap keys</strong> — bind <span class="gx-key">W</span><span class="gx-key">A</span><span class="gx-key">S</span><span class="gx-key">D</span> to the D-pad in the emulator's key editor. 30 seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Plug in a controller</strong> — Xbox/PS pad works as a D-pad out of the box.</li>
        <li><strong>Fn layer</strong> — most 60% boards put arrows on <span class="gx-key">Fn</span>+WASD or IJKL.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>🚀 Make it run its best</h2>
    <div class="gx-tips">
      <div class="gx-tip"><span class="gx-tip__i">⚙️</span><div><strong>Enable virtualization.</strong> Biggest single win. Stutter = this is off. Turn on VT-x / AMD-V in BIOS.</div></div>
      <div class="gx-tip"><span class="gx-tip__i">🧠</span><div><strong>Give it room.</strong> 2–4 CPU cores and 2–4 GB RAM in the emulator's settings is plenty.</div></div>
      <div class="gx-tip"><span class="gx-tip__i">🔄</span><div><strong>Leave the updater on.</strong> The app keeps itself current, so you sideload once and forget it.</div></div>
      <div class="gx-tip"><span class="gx-tip__i">🚫</span><div><strong>Keep "Block ads in player" ON</strong> for ad-free YouTube/Twitch. Only flip it off if a movie loads to a white screen.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2>🩹 Stuck? Quick fixes</h2>
    <div class="gx-fixes">
      <div class="gx-fix">
        <span class="gx-fix__p">Nothing plays</span>
        <span class="gx-fix__f">Try another title or "Choose server" — usually one dead mirror, not you. If <em>everything</em> fails, set your DNS to <strong>1.1.1.1</strong>.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-fix">
        <span class="gx-fix__p">No icon after install</span>
        <span class="gx-fix__f">Wait a few seconds, check the app drawer. Still missing? Use the <strong>Install APK</strong> button instead of drag-drop.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="gx-fix">
        <span class="gx-fix__p">Feels slow</span>
        <span class="gx-fix__f">Virtualization is off, or it's starved. Enable VT-x / AMD-V and give it a couple cores.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="gx-outro">
      <strong>Real talk:</strong> the Android TV build is free and sideloaded, with no account required and
      history kept on the device. An emulator adds a little overhead versus a real box, while the PC web beta
      is the quickest way to try it in a browser. Own a VSeeBox or SuperBox? Use the APK on the couch. Don't?
      The beta gets the experience onto the screen you already own. 🎉
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
